  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m / [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — CommonJS, unrelated to the page; [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no deps and no scripts.
  - Two module conventions already coexist (script-tag globals vs CommonJS), which is the real decision here.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the page picks up settings determines the module format, and that's the part that's expensive to change
  later.

  - [1mSecond [38;5;153m<script src="settings.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing a global.[0m Buys: zero new tooling, matches how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
    already loads, works by opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m from disk. Costs: settings live on a global ([38;5;153mwindow.APP_CONFIG[39m); no
    import graph. Rules out nothing permanently — but doesn't help [38;5;153msrc/[39m reuse the config.
  - [1mES modules ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mexport[39m/[38;5;153mimport[39m).[0m Buys: real imports, explicit dependencies, the modern default.
    Costs: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m both change; [38;5;153mfile://[39m opening breaks (needs a local server) — that's the sharp edge.
    Rules in a cleaner path to sharing config with [38;5;153msrc/[39m later.
  - [1mBuild step / bundler that injects env vars.[0m Buys: real per-environment builds, secrets kept out of source. Costs: a
    dependency and a toolchain in a repo that currently has neither. Hardest to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation: the plain script-tag global.[0m It fits the file that actually has the constant, keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m
  working, and "easier to change environments" here means "edit one obvious place," which it fully delivers. ES modules
  are the better answer only if you intend [38;5;153msrc/[39m and the page to share code — worth saying now, since retrofitting is
  cheap but rewriting twice isn't.
